Reawakening OS 0.3.0-r13 — installer, administrator access, Cockpit, real amplification

file:   reawakening-os-0.3.0-r13-amd64.iso
size:   1176059904 bytes
sha256: 40ed8f124f29d3f5c7f3f926b78617b3324df982df96ca833d41015e3c9130b9

Reawakening OS installs to a disk for the first time.

- refurb-install runs in the live session (launcher entry, `sudo refurb-install`
  in a terminal, or with a preselected /dev target). It lists every disk with
  size, model and internal/removable, refuses the disk backing the live medium,
  prints the target's current partitions, and changes nothing until the user
  types exactly `erase <disk>`. It then creates a GPT layout (UEFI: 512 MB FAT32
  ESP + ext4 root; BIOS: 2 MB BIOS boot + ext4 root), unpacks the live squashfs,
  writes UUID fstab/hostname/hosts, creates the chosen administrator account
  (username, full name, password typed twice, sudo group), optionally sets a
  root password and otherwise leaves root locked, removes the live account and
  the live passwordless-sudo rule, deletes the installer from the target,
  rebuilds the initramfs, installs GRUB and offers a reboot. Desktop settings,
  wallpaper choice and saved NetworkManager connections are carried into the
  new home. A USB target gets GRUB's removable path (/EFI/BOOT on its own ESP,
  no NVRAM entry), and every UEFI install writes that fallback so a disk boots
  even when the firmware has no boot variable; an internal UEFI disk also
  attempts a normal firmware entry. Only the selected disk is touched.

- The live medium is administrable. Its account has no password by design
  (tty1 autologins it), so r13 grants that account passwordless sudo via
  /etc/sudoers.d/50-refurb-live, validated with visudo at build time. The
  installer deletes the file from installed systems, where sudo uses the
  account's own password. Root is never given a default password.

- Cockpit remote access is repaired. The shipped loopback drop-in was named
  listen.conf, which sorted after the 50-refurb-remote.conf written by
  `refurb-cockpit on`, so the socket restarted back onto 127.0.0.1 and the
  switch did nothing. It is now 10-refurb-loopback.conf; the remote drop-in is
  read last. URLs printed by the script are https://, `refurb-cockpit ip`
  prints the address to use, `refurb-cockpit password` works on the
  passwordless live account (sudo passwd), and iproute2 is installed so ip/ss
  exist. The installer enables cockpit.socket on the target and, unless
  declined, allows the local network.

- Audio above 100% is real gain. PipeWire's default hardware mixer cannot go
  past the codec maximum, so the bar moved while the speaker level did not.
  WirePlumber now sets api.alsa.soft-mixer = true for ALSA cards and keeps the
  hardware mixer at 0 dB, with refurb-audio normalising Master/PCM/Speaker once
  per session (Headphone/Headset untouched). The idle-suspend rule now also
  matches the SOF/UCM `HiFi__Speaker` sink used by many Chromebooks, not only
  the HDA analog name. rtkit is installed so PipeWire gets real-time priority
  instead of logging RTKit errors.

Verified in QEMU (2 GB, 2 cores, software emulation), from this ISO:

- Full BIOS install to a blank 8 GB virtio disk, then boot of that disk with no
  ISO attached: custom desktop drawn, testuser at uid 1000, live account gone,
  sudo id -u = 0, root locked, expected fstab/hostname/kernel/initramfs/GRUB,
  sudoers holding only the two helpers, installer absent from the target.
- File persistence across a reboot and the restored 160% sink volume.
- Full UEFI install and boot of the disk under OVMF. The ESP holds both
  /EFI/BOOT/BOOTX64.EFI and /EFI/reawakening/grubx64.efi, and the disk also
  boots with a fresh, empty OVMF variable store through the fallback.
- External-USB install: a marked internal disk (GPT, FAKEWIN vFAT, marker.txt)
  was byte-identical after installing to a USB-attached disk (first 64 MB
  SHA256 unchanged, marker readable, partition table unchanged); the USB disk
  then booted alone with the account, sudo and Cockpit working.
- Live Cockpit switch: `refurb-cockpit on` listened on *:9090 and the host got
  200 (/), 401 (/cockpit/login without credentials); `refurb-cockpit off`
  returned the socket to 127.0.0.1 and the host stopped connecting; the root
  invocation `sudo refurb-cockpit on` also works now.
- Cockpit from the host (a different machine): GET / 200, /cockpit/login 401
  without credentials, HTTP Basic login 200 with the installed account.
- Audio mechanism: api.alsa.soft-mixer true, node.suspend-on-idle false,
  Master at [100%] [0.00dB], `wpctl set-volume --limit 1.6 ... 160%` reports
  1.60 with the hardware mixer unchanged, mute/unmute works.
- scripts/check-config.sh passes. No shell source changed this round; cargo is
  not installed in this build session, so the 35 shell tests were not re-run
  (their last run was on identical shell code).

Not verified: loudness, distortion, headphones, Jellyfin playback and the
Chromebook's actual codec controls; Cockpit's browser UI and web terminal (the
HTTPS/PAM layer was verified with curl, not clicked through); installation on
physical firmware, real USB enumeration and an existing Windows ESP; Wi-Fi
settings carried through a real install (the copy is implemented; there is no
radio in the emulated machine).
